Let's knock out the fourth and final bottleneck: **SQL Injection & Cache Eviction in the Data Lake** within **HdfsSyncService.java**.
In big data environments like Spark and Hive, query performance is heavily reliant on "Execution Plans." When you use String.format to hardcode values directly into the SQL string (e.g., VALUES ('10301INRMEMO', ...)), Spark sees it as a brand-new query every single time. It has to pause, parse the string, and calculate a completely new execution plan for every row.
By switching to parameterized queries (using ?), Spark caches the execution plan once and reuses it for every row, massively speeding up your sync process while simultaneously protecting you against SQL injection.
### The Fix
Open **HdfsSyncService.java**. We are going to move the SQL string *outside* of the for loop so it's only created once, and replace the hardcoded values with ? parameters.
Replace your entire class with this optimized version:
```java
package com.fincore.VoucherService.Service.external;

import com.fincore.VoucherService.Dto.HdfsSyncDto;
import lombok.RequiredArgsConstructor;
import lombok.extern.slf4j.Slf4j;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Qualifier;
import org.springframework.dao.DataAccessException;
import org.springframework.jdbc.core.JdbcTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

import java.util.List;

/**
 * Service responsible for synchronizing aggregated GL balances to the HDFS Data Lake.
 * Implements native Delta Lake MERGE INTO logic for atomic, duplicate-free Upserts.
 */
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
@Slf4j
public class HdfsSyncService {

    @Autowired
    @Qualifier("hiveJdbcTemplate")
    private JdbcTemplate hiveJdbcTemplate;

    public void syncToDataLake(List<HdfsSyncDto> data) {
        if (data == null || data.isEmpty()) return;

        log.info("HDFS_SYNC_START | Target: Hive Data Lake | RecordCount: {}", data.size());
        long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();

        try {
            // 1. DEFINE SQL OUTSIDE THE LOOP (Allows Spark/Hive to cache the execution plan)
            String mergeSql = "MERGE INTO fincoredatalakejournal.gl_balance AS target " +
                    "USING (SELECT ? AS glcc, cast(? AS date) AS balance_date, ? AS closing_balance, ? AS inr_balance) AS source " +
                    "ON target.glcc = source.glcc AND target.BALANCE_DATE = source.balance_date " +
                    "WHEN MATCHED THEN " +
                    "  UPDATE SET target.closing_balance = source.closing_balance, target.INR_BALANCE = source.inr_balance " +
                    "WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN " +
                    "  INSERT (glcc, BALANCE_DATE, closing_balance, INR_BALANCE) " +
                    "  VALUES (source.glcc, source.balance_date, source.closing_balance, source.inr_balance)";

            for (HdfsSyncDto dto : data) {
                String glccKey = dto.getBranch() + dto.getCurrency() + dto.getCgl();

                // 2. USE PARAMETERIZED UPDATE (Protects against SQL Injection & boosts speed)
                hiveJdbcTemplate.update(
                        mergeSql,
                        glccKey,
                        dto.getBalanceDate().toString(),
                        dto.getNewBalance(), // JdbcTemplate natively handles BigDecimal
                        dto.getNewInrBalance()
                );
            }

            log.info("HDFS_SYNC_SUCCESS | Duration: {}ms | Records: {}",
                    (System.currentTimeMillis() - startTime), data.size());

        } catch (DataAccessException e) {
            // Extract the exact Spark Thrift Server error message for cleaner debugging
            Throwable rootCause = e.getRootCause();
            String exactSparkError = (rootCause != null) ? rootCause.getMessage() : e.getMessage();

            log.error("HDFS_SYNC_DB_FAIL | Spark Error Details: {}", exactSparkError);
            throw e;
        }
    }
}

```
### Why this is a massive upgrade:
 1. **Speed:** JdbcTemplate automatically maps BigDecimal objects straight into your Hive database, saving Java the effort of converting numbers to strings and back again.
 2. **Security:** No one can ever break your Hive syntax by accidentally (or maliciously) putting a single quote (') in a branch code or currency.
And with that, your entire validation architecture, async scheduling, database locking, and HDFS syncing are completely hardened for a heavy-load production environment.
